invariant: INV-17
file: sha256_hw.c
catches: test/docker-aes-runtime-qemu.sh sha2-equiv
reason: kept reads the state a block began with through a plain pointer, so
  the compiler may hold that state in a register across the block's
  sixty-four rounds. x86-64 has 16 vector registers, and gcc 13 then puts
  one half in a stack slot of its own, outside the block_state, where the
  wipe does not clear it: after the first block of an HMAC that half
  stands for the key. Every digest is still right. bin/sha2_equiv_test
  copies the stack below each kind of call and looks for four state words
  in a row, and the script builds it for x86-64 with the container's gcc,
  the compiler and the target the slot appears under. arm64 has 32 vector
  registers and no such slot, so the same binary on an arm64 host passes.
  The lane skips without docker, and this then reports unguarded rather
  than caught.

--- old
    const volatile words *in_state = field;
--- new
    const words *in_state = field;
